- It is not a programming language in the conventional sense.
  It has no instructions, control flow, syntax order, or side effects.

- It is not a natural language.
  It has no lexicon, grammar, or dialogue semantics in the human sense.

- It is not a logic system based on binary truth tables.
  Truth is a geometric property, not a discrete proposition.

- It is not a knowledge graph or ontology in the conventional sense.
  Relations are geometric curvatures, not edges.

- It is not merely a vector embedding space.
  Embeddings are finite approximations of a deeper continuous manifold.

- It is not directly human-readable.
  Human-readable exposition is only a projection layer, not the object itself.

These are not limitations; they are the design constraints that make LangOne fundamentally different from contemporary representational systems.

